From 0746512bd37bf47d92c7d7633a28245e327a1bd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:54:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add server-side push notification sending via Firebase Cloud
 Messaging
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Client-side push registration already existed (native-app.ts syncs an
FCM token to /api/push-devices), and the schema already had a
pushNotifications preference column, but nothing on the server ever
actually sent a push — the routes only stored/deleted device tokens.
Android builds also had no google-services.json, so the Gradle
google-services plugin was never applied and FCM couldn't initialize
in the app at all.

- New artifacts/api-server/src/lib/push.ts: sendPushNotification(),
  gated on FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64 (base64-encoded Firebase
  service account key, same rationale as the base64-encoded Android
  keystore already used in CI — avoids env var mangling the multi-line
  private_key field). Sends via firebase-admin's sendEachForMulticast
  to every device registered for a user, and self-heals by deleting
  tokens FCM reports as no-longer-registered.
- Wired into createNotification() (lib/notifications.ts), the shared
  funnel most notification call sites already use, so every one of
  those gets push for free.
- eventChangeNotifications.ts has its own bespoke bulk delivery path
  and already gated an "inApp" channel on user.pushNotifications
  without ever sending a real push — added the actual send there.
- volunteerTasks.ts and volunteerReminders.ts did raw
  db.insert(notificationsTable) calls bypassing createNotification
  entirely (and its preference checks); switched both to
  createNotification() so they pick up push (and proper
  notificationsEnabled/pushNotifications gating) for free.
- Both Android GitHub Actions workflows now write google-services.json
  from a new GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON_BASE64 secret before building, same
  pattern as the existing keystore decode step. Safe to leave unset —
  logs a message and builds without push support, same as today.
- .env.example documents the new env var.

Still needed before this actually delivers notifications: a Firebase
project (Console → Add app → app.trailteam.trailteam), its
google-services.json set as GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON_BASE64, and a service
account key set as FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64 on the Replit
deployment.

